1
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:02,190
Ikuru.

2
00:00:02,190 --> 00:00:03,110
Rinka?

3
00:00:03,110 --> 00:00:04,850
Who's this?

4
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:06,630
Iroha Nashiki.

5
00:00:06,630 --> 00:00:11,570
If you're Ikuru's friend,
I'd like to be friends, too.

6
00:00:42,630 --> 00:00:44,370
What's wrong?

7
00:01:05,150 --> 00:01:06,950
Is something the matter?

8
00:01:07,850 --> 00:01:09,900
No.

9
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:13,630
Guess I had too much to drink.

10
00:01:13,630 --> 00:01:15,730
You did.

11
00:01:15,730 --> 00:01:18,800
Don't ever go drinking on your own.

12
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:19,770
Okay?

13
00:01:19,770 --> 00:01:20,890
Why not?

14
00:01:20,890 --> 00:01:22,580
Because you'll expose yourself again.

15
00:01:22,580 --> 00:01:25,860
I was only belly dancing.

16
00:01:27,870 --> 00:01:30,970
That doesn't fly at drinking parties now.

17
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:34,290
Mind if I stop by the store?

18
00:01:34,290 --> 00:01:35,830
I want to buy bread for tomorrow.

19
00:01:35,830 --> 00:01:37,620
Sure.

20
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:40,820
- Will a simple loaf do?
- Yeah.

21
00:01:53,850 --> 00:01:56,230
We're back.

22
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:07,400
- We're home.
- Hey.

23
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:09,080
Persistent bastard...

24
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:10,780
How can you enjoy these gory games?

25
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:14,540
Agh, I definitely had him!
[Game Over]

26
00:02:14,540 --> 00:02:15,740
You have poor taste.

27
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:18,320
Zombies died once already,
so why kill them again?

28
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:21,310
Your definition of a zombie is so old-school.

29
00:02:21,310 --> 00:02:22,410
Really?

30
00:02:22,410 --> 00:02:25,660
Some people get turned
while they're still alive.

31
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:29,140
Whether that be via virus, parasite,
rebirth, failing nerves,

32
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:31,990
it's all different.

33
00:02:31,990 --> 00:02:35,570
If anything, the dead-come-alive type
is a rarity nowadays.

34
00:02:35,570 --> 00:02:37,230
I see.

35
00:02:38,370 --> 00:02:42,120
I need to get back soon myself.

36
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:46,320
Definitely. Your friends probably
all think you're dead.

37
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:48,180
What if they've started the funeral already?

38
00:02:48,180 --> 00:02:49,190
- Hey.
- Holy cow.

39
00:02:49,190 --> 00:02:53,030
They'd be so excited if Iroha
suddenly reappeared out of nowhere!

40
00:02:53,030 --> 00:02:57,200
The idea of time travelers
didn't exist back then,

41
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:01,930
so they'll make a fuss about her
returning from the dead!

42
00:03:01,930 --> 00:03:03,510
I'm heading to bed.

43
00:03:03,510 --> 00:03:04,940
Sure.

44
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:18,750
Ikuru.

45
00:03:18,750 --> 00:03:21,940
Iroha will find her way home, right?

46
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:23,330
You were confident she would.

47
00:03:23,330 --> 00:03:25,720
I know, but...

48
00:03:25,720 --> 00:03:29,390
I guess that means goodbye
when the August meteor shower hits.

49
00:03:29,390 --> 00:03:35,080
Hey, if it doesn't work out,
she can stay here, right?

50
00:03:36,930 --> 00:03:38,870
Right.

51
00:03:38,870 --> 00:03:40,610
Well, time to get back to it.

52
00:03:40,610 --> 00:03:43,110
Ease up on the gaming, okay?

53
00:03:43,110 --> 00:03:45,980
Use that smart noggin of yours for something.

54
00:03:45,980 --> 00:03:49,130
Watch your mouth, big bro.

55
00:03:49,130 --> 00:03:50,790
What's with the attitude?

56
00:03:50,790 --> 00:03:54,370
I'm about to make it big.

57
00:03:54,370 --> 00:03:55,740
See?

58
00:03:56,970 --> 00:03:58,030
What am I looking at?

59
00:03:58,030 --> 00:04:01,220
I'm ranked 8th, with 30 million clicks.

60
00:04:01,220 --> 00:04:04,920
"I Met a Zombie the Day I Was Rejected,

61
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:08,320
but Honestly, it's Going Better With Her".

62
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:10,070
- I based the protagonist off of you.
- What?

63
00:04:10,070 --> 00:04:11,600
I think it'll get published eventually!

64
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:13,530
Hey! I object to the title!

65
00:04:13,530 --> 00:04:15,530
- Delete that! Delete it!
- Woah! No way!

66
00:04:15,530 --> 00:04:18,150
- Saya!
- Nope! Not happening!

67
00:04:18,150 --> 00:04:20,900
Give me your phone!

68
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:27,170
Not a scratch in sight.

69
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:33,650
I should really hold back on the drinks.

70
00:04:39,180 --> 00:04:42,040
I will return to my Shibuya Village.

71
00:04:43,030 --> 00:04:47,210
I was only sent here because of some mix-up.

72
00:04:50,570 --> 00:04:52,460
Right.

73
00:04:52,460 --> 00:04:55,630
I still have Master Seki's question to solve.

74
00:04:56,590 --> 00:04:59,040
I'll get back and surprise him,
answer in tow.

75
00:05:08,470 --> 00:05:10,110
What's wrong?

76
00:05:17,090 --> 00:05:18,930
Everything is okay.

77
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:35,770
30 million clicks, on this?

78
00:05:41,980 --> 00:05:44,690
<i>And it ranks 8th in the romance category?</i>

79
00:05:44,690 --> 00:05:46,190
<i>Kill me now!</i>

80
00:05:46,190 --> 00:05:48,490
She's got zombies and vampires all mixed up.

81
00:05:48,490 --> 00:05:51,510
Damn it, Saya.
Don't write something so embarrassing!

82
00:05:55,050 --> 00:05:58,280
I have to graduate from
the "single for life" status, stat.

83
00:05:58,280 --> 00:05:59,940
Alright.

84
00:06:02,540 --> 00:06:04,200
Mr. Konishi?

85
00:06:06,180 --> 00:06:08,150
This is Ichinose, speaking.

86
00:06:08,150 --> 00:06:12,020
<i>Mr. Ichinose, bad news!</i>

87
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:13,560
What happened?

88
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:18,450
<i>Our school is being put on blast
for a sticker theft incident!</i>

89
00:06:18,450 --> 00:06:21,610
- What?
- <i>I'll send you the URL!</i>

90
00:06:21,610 --> 00:06:22,920
Please do.

91
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:28,380
[Yuto Konishi]

92
00:06:29,740 --> 00:06:32,890
[My third grader's stickers were stolen]

93
00:06:32,890 --> 00:06:36,050
[The school won't do anything about it]

94
00:06:36,050 --> 00:06:37,440
What?

95
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:40,150
[View quote reposts]

96
00:06:40,150 --> 00:06:44,080
[Quote reposts]

97
00:06:46,290 --> 00:06:48,590
[The school isn't taking appropriate action]

98
00:06:48,590 --> 00:06:50,700
[They're not protecting our children]

99
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:53,390
[What are you teaching the kids? LOL]
[Only more troubles will follow]

100
00:06:54,070 --> 00:06:56,370
What the hell?

101
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:08,040
Mom.

102
00:07:08,750 --> 00:07:10,680
You're still awake?

103
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:12,340
I can't sleep.

104
00:07:12,340 --> 00:07:14,810
Just close your eyes.

105
00:07:14,810 --> 00:07:18,480
Where's Dad? Still at work?

106
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:20,050
Yeah.

107
00:07:23,300 --> 00:07:26,040
[Best wishes in your new house!]

108
00:07:26,750 --> 00:07:30,330
Yes. Yes, of course. Have a good day.

109
00:07:34,530 --> 00:07:37,840
- Shibuya Third, how may we help?
- This is just ridiculous.

110
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:40,870
This post has a million views
over some stupid stickers!

111
00:07:40,870 --> 00:07:43,700
Because it serves as fuel against schools.

112
00:07:43,700 --> 00:07:46,960
I can't believe some politician
has even quoted the post.

113
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:49,860
Quit using us to promote your agenda.

114
00:07:49,860 --> 00:07:53,540
If anything, it's the parents' fault

115
00:07:53,540 --> 00:07:56,480
for letting their kids
bring these things to school.

116
00:07:56,480 --> 00:07:59,560
- Exactly.
- As I have already said...

117
00:07:59,560 --> 00:08:01,380
I mean.

118
00:08:01,380 --> 00:08:02,790
Yes.

119
00:08:02,790 --> 00:08:06,270
I feel especially bad
for the third grade teachers...

120
00:08:10,090 --> 00:08:11,970
Shibuya Third, how may we help?

121
00:08:11,970 --> 00:08:16,650
None of you knew of this incident beforehand?

122
00:08:16,650 --> 00:08:20,130
Yes. My students made no mention of it.

123
00:08:20,130 --> 00:08:22,080
Likewise.

124
00:08:25,270 --> 00:08:28,570
We were aware of stickers being brought in.

125
00:08:28,570 --> 00:08:33,090
And I have seen kids fight
over rare stickers as well.

126
00:08:33,090 --> 00:08:36,390
- But theft...
- Stop.

127
00:08:36,940 --> 00:08:42,430
First, let's make sure
we're all on the same page.

128
00:08:43,520 --> 00:08:51,140
No theft occurred at our school.

129
00:08:51,140 --> 00:08:52,480
Pardon?

130
00:08:54,450 --> 00:08:57,660
We're gathered here to talk
because something was stolen, right?

131
00:08:57,660 --> 00:09:00,120
No, Ms. Nashiki.

132
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:03,300
A student simply lost their item

133
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:08,650
within the school.

134
00:09:08,650 --> 00:09:12,210
Lost, and not stolen?

135
00:09:12,210 --> 00:09:15,970
That's right. The stickers went missing.

136
00:09:15,970 --> 00:09:19,850
We, the school, are not to blame.

137
00:09:19,850 --> 00:09:23,800
This has merely become an incident

138
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:26,260
because others are making a fuss about it.

139
00:09:26,260 --> 00:09:28,800
But the student is saying
their belongings were stolen.

140
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:32,640
And how do we prove that?

141
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:34,790
Prove it?

142
00:09:36,120 --> 00:09:40,740
Us faculty aren't like police,
who can do a thorough investigation.

143
00:09:40,740 --> 00:09:45,350
Legally, we aren't allowed to inspect
our students' belongings.

144
00:09:45,350 --> 00:09:46,850
Really?

145
00:09:46,850 --> 00:09:50,440
If we did, we would be violating
our students' right to privacy.

146
00:09:50,440 --> 00:09:51,700
All we can do,

147
00:09:51,700 --> 00:09:55,690
is request that each student
check their belongings once more.

148
00:09:55,690 --> 00:09:57,870
But...

149
00:09:58,620 --> 00:10:00,870
They could lie.

150
00:10:00,870 --> 00:10:04,040
Yes, they can.

151
00:10:04,640 --> 00:10:07,010
And we're aware it will happen.

152
00:10:07,010 --> 00:10:12,420
Even so, we can't create a culprit
within our classrooms.

153
00:10:14,050 --> 00:10:18,690
In this case, the student's parent
made a post about the incident online,

154
00:10:18,690 --> 00:10:22,480
even though the student themselves
didn't come to us teachers for help.

155
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:25,360
It truly is the worst scenario to deal with.

156
00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:29,640
Still, we cannot let this shake us.

157
00:10:29,640 --> 00:10:31,330
But you said we'd look into it...

158
00:10:31,330 --> 00:10:33,670
With how the story has spread,

159
00:10:33,670 --> 00:10:37,750
the public now sees that post as the truth.

160
00:10:37,750 --> 00:10:41,060
So to appease the masses,
we'll say we're looking into it.

161
00:10:41,060 --> 00:10:45,050
But in reality, we won't be?

162
00:10:45,950 --> 00:10:47,590
Exactly.

163
00:10:47,590 --> 00:10:49,970
No such theft occurred, after all.

164
00:10:49,970 --> 00:10:54,020
That is how we as a school
must protect ourselves.

165
00:10:54,020 --> 00:10:59,960
We will deal with this incident
like any other lost items case.

166
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:01,340
Understood.

167
00:11:01,340 --> 00:11:03,800
I will make sure my class is informed.

168
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:05,820
I will inform the parents as well.

169
00:11:05,820 --> 00:11:08,410
Make sure your students
also reassess their belongings.

170
00:11:08,410 --> 00:11:10,670
That will be all.

171
00:11:10,670 --> 00:11:11,650
Yes, ma'am.

172
00:11:11,650 --> 00:11:14,540
Good. Now let us get back to work.

173
00:11:23,470 --> 00:11:25,560
Ms. Principal.

174
00:11:25,560 --> 00:11:28,500
I don't understand.

175
00:11:29,080 --> 00:11:33,720
We're lying to the parents,
and turning a blind eye to the kids.

176
00:11:33,720 --> 00:11:36,270
So we're deceiving the both of them?

177
00:11:37,330 --> 00:11:39,910
You said something about not creating
a culprit in the classroom.

178
00:11:39,910 --> 00:11:45,410
Does that mean we're letting the thief go?

179
00:11:46,510 --> 00:11:53,100
Will that child be reprimanded by no one?

180
00:11:53,100 --> 00:11:55,560
What is it you're trying to say?

181
00:11:57,210 --> 00:12:02,670
Does complacency truly help the guilty child?

182
00:12:04,210 --> 00:12:06,510
Yes, we are looking into it.

183
00:12:06,510 --> 00:12:09,010
Have a good day.

184
00:12:11,270 --> 00:12:13,990
"To overlook evil is not compassion".

185
00:12:13,990 --> 00:12:19,360
Is it not our job to correct their wrongs?

186
00:12:25,120 --> 00:12:29,130
What era do you think this is?

187
00:12:29,130 --> 00:12:32,190
There are no witnesses,
no security camera footage,

188
00:12:32,190 --> 00:12:33,860
and we can't check their belongings.

189
00:12:33,860 --> 00:12:35,650
So who do we make the culprit?

190
00:12:35,650 --> 00:12:38,650
But my master said it is our duty to...

191
00:12:38,650 --> 00:12:40,770
You're being too idealistic.

192
00:13:11,040 --> 00:13:12,790
Ms. Nashiki.

193
00:13:14,870 --> 00:13:16,490
Brighten up.

194
00:13:23,100 --> 00:13:25,210
Does it still bother you?

195
00:13:30,310 --> 00:13:34,170
Teaching is an important job.

196
00:13:34,170 --> 00:13:40,280
But school is no place for a teacher
to become so attached to her students.

197
00:13:42,540 --> 00:13:44,940
- Why not?
- Just listen.

198
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:48,740
A mistake I make
isn't solely my responsibility.

199
00:13:48,740 --> 00:13:50,720
It becomes the responsibility
of the entire school.

200
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:55,410
But you said you wanted to protect them.

201
00:13:55,410 --> 00:13:59,220
<i>That's why I choose to accomplish
the bare minimum every day.</i>

202
00:13:59,220 --> 00:14:01,740
<i>The bare minimum?</i>

203
00:14:01,740 --> 00:14:05,620
<i>To protect the lives of my students.</i>

204
00:14:05,620 --> 00:14:07,600
Were you lying?

205
00:14:07,600 --> 00:14:11,880
Protecting your students means
treating their lives like your own.

206
00:14:11,880 --> 00:14:13,130
I can't overlook the issue.

207
00:14:13,130 --> 00:14:15,670
But there is something you can do.

208
00:14:17,160 --> 00:14:19,970
Your classroom is your territory.

209
00:14:19,970 --> 00:14:23,550
You can make a change,
despite the limitations.

210
00:14:29,320 --> 00:14:31,030
If only...

211
00:14:32,550 --> 00:14:36,070
If only I were Ms. Nashiki.

212
00:14:37,490 --> 00:14:38,810
Huh?

213
00:14:43,200 --> 00:14:49,070
<i>She had always called
our current era, "strange".</i>

214
00:14:49,070 --> 00:14:53,250
<i>Yet despite the disconnect,
she always chose to enjoy herself.</i>

215
00:14:53,880 --> 00:14:58,040
<i>That's how she had managed to overcome
the wall separating our eras.</i>

216
00:14:59,720 --> 00:15:06,390
<i>But now, she's hurt.</i>

217
00:15:06,390 --> 00:15:11,680
<i>She's hurt by the differences
between our two eras.</i>

218
00:15:22,750 --> 00:15:26,030
[Episode 6]

219
00:15:26,030 --> 00:15:28,220
Guys, big news!

220
00:15:28,220 --> 00:15:29,630
Did you see that post?

221
00:15:29,630 --> 00:15:31,410
Apparently that's about us!

222
00:15:31,410 --> 00:15:33,550
Does that mean one of our parents wrote it?

223
00:15:33,550 --> 00:15:36,620
We shouldn't be bringing stickers
in the first place.

224
00:15:36,620 --> 00:15:38,880
Was that post about you, Ichika?

225
00:15:38,880 --> 00:15:41,310
Someone stole your Ramunen stickers, right?

226
00:15:41,310 --> 00:15:44,060
Who would do that?

227
00:15:44,060 --> 00:15:48,510
Midori, you were the last one out
the other day.

228
00:15:48,510 --> 00:15:50,630
Did you...

229
00:15:52,950 --> 00:15:55,710
- Morning.
- Good morning, Ms. Nashiki.

230
00:15:55,710 --> 00:15:57,970
Get to your seats, everyone.

231
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:09,070
What's wrong, Ms. Nashiki?

232
00:16:12,040 --> 00:16:13,790
Well...

233
00:16:14,790 --> 00:16:20,050
I have something to tell you all
before we start class today.

234
00:16:25,570 --> 00:16:30,120
In life, there are bad things people can do.

235
00:16:30,120 --> 00:16:35,220
But there's rarely ever a bad person.

236
00:16:36,930 --> 00:16:39,810
People are sometimes led astray.

237
00:16:39,810 --> 00:16:43,140
Sometimes, their hearts waver.

238
00:16:44,680 --> 00:16:47,260
And sometimes, this leads people to do things

239
00:16:47,260 --> 00:16:50,380
they never wanted to.

240
00:16:50,980 --> 00:16:56,020
Ignoring such wavering hearts
is something I can't allow myself to do.

241
00:16:56,830 --> 00:17:00,280
If you've lost yourself
to your wavering heart,

242
00:17:00,280 --> 00:17:03,220
then I want to help out.

243
00:17:03,220 --> 00:17:07,430
If you feel guilt inside your heart,

244
00:17:07,430 --> 00:17:10,010
then that means it's not too late.

245
00:17:10,760 --> 00:17:15,430
I can place your lost heart,
right back where it belongs.

246
00:17:16,590 --> 00:17:19,460
Human beings may be weak.

247
00:17:20,350 --> 00:17:22,170
But they're also strong.

248
00:17:22,170 --> 00:17:24,980
So, class.

249
00:17:25,710 --> 00:17:29,970
If anything's bothering you,
come talk to me, alright?

250
00:17:32,650 --> 00:17:35,970
Now let's get started with today's lesson.

251
00:17:35,970 --> 00:17:38,510
Yes, Ms. Nashiki.

252
00:17:46,540 --> 00:17:48,870
<i>You said something about not creating
a culprit in the classroom.</i>

253
00:17:48,870 --> 00:17:52,550
<i>Does that mean we're letting the thief go?</i>

254
00:18:04,450 --> 00:18:06,550
Oh, it's just you, Ikuru.

255
00:18:06,550 --> 00:18:08,070
What's up?

256
00:18:11,910 --> 00:18:13,980
Watch and learn.

257
00:18:15,340 --> 00:18:17,930
Stand back.

258
00:18:17,930 --> 00:18:19,420
Ready?

259
00:18:22,630 --> 00:18:23,890
Your turn.

260
00:18:23,890 --> 00:18:25,090
Huh?

261
00:18:25,090 --> 00:18:27,630
You're the daughter of a courier group. Jump.

262
00:18:27,630 --> 00:18:29,250
What's that got to do with anything?

263
00:18:29,250 --> 00:18:32,030
Go on, jump.

264
00:18:32,030 --> 00:18:33,780
You wanna see me jump?

265
00:18:42,340 --> 00:18:44,330
See? Easy.

266
00:18:44,330 --> 00:18:45,710
Don't get so cocky.

267
00:18:45,710 --> 00:18:48,100
This is the height for fourth graders.

268
00:18:48,100 --> 00:18:53,190
Then why'd you make me jump it?

269
00:19:18,350 --> 00:19:20,570
You're allowed to stay.

270
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:26,720
If something's on your mind, just hop around.

271
00:19:26,720 --> 00:19:28,520
Like any courier would, right?

272
00:19:33,550 --> 00:19:35,240
You're making no sense.

273
00:19:35,240 --> 00:19:37,300
And you're making fun of couriers.

274
00:19:37,300 --> 00:19:40,840
I would never.
I'm still impressed by your run.

275
00:19:40,840 --> 00:19:43,100
You are!

276
00:19:43,100 --> 00:19:44,590
My Tokubei gang...

277
00:19:44,590 --> 00:19:46,670
Tokubei gang?

278
00:19:46,670 --> 00:19:48,420
Mr. Majima!

279
00:19:50,060 --> 00:19:52,230
I get it now.

280
00:19:52,230 --> 00:19:54,740
No, I was just measuring her height.

281
00:19:54,740 --> 00:19:57,650
So that's your secret, Ms. Nashiki.

282
00:19:57,650 --> 00:19:58,660
My secret?

283
00:19:58,660 --> 00:19:59,910
Mr. Majima...

284
00:19:59,910 --> 00:20:03,590
The reason why Ms. Nashiki
seemingly became a different person.

285
00:20:03,590 --> 00:20:06,050
The reason why you two are so close now.

286
00:20:06,050 --> 00:20:11,380
It's all because Mr. Ichinose
found out your identity, isn't it?

287
00:20:12,390 --> 00:20:13,880
My identity?

288
00:20:13,880 --> 00:20:15,640
Well, well...

289
00:20:15,640 --> 00:20:19,360
That's why you quit acting.

290
00:20:19,360 --> 00:20:21,110
I'm right, aren't I?

291
00:20:22,250 --> 00:20:23,800
Sorry, what are you...

292
00:20:23,800 --> 00:20:25,580
Don't try to fool me.

293
00:20:25,580 --> 00:20:28,280
Besides, I'm not prejudiced.

294
00:20:28,280 --> 00:20:30,910
Only 'your' history and criminal record

295
00:20:30,910 --> 00:20:33,450
are considered when becoming a teacher.

296
00:20:33,450 --> 00:20:34,290
Huh?

297
00:20:34,290 --> 00:20:39,550
I don't care whatever gang
you're the daughter of.

298
00:20:40,100 --> 00:20:43,220
Mr. Ichinose isn't the only one
who understands you.

299
00:20:43,220 --> 00:20:46,290
If anything, I'm prepared to overcome...

300
00:20:47,220 --> 00:20:53,180
<i>Ms. Nashiki, Mr. Majima.
Please come to the faculty room immediately.</i>

301
00:20:58,670 --> 00:21:01,010
What is it, Ms. Akutsu?

302
00:21:01,850 --> 00:21:05,040
I've gathered the other third grade teachers.

303
00:21:05,040 --> 00:21:06,990
Excuse us.

304
00:21:06,990 --> 00:21:11,520
The President of the PTA is coming
to speak with us about you-know-what.

305
00:21:11,520 --> 00:21:12,860
What?

306
00:21:12,860 --> 00:21:15,320
Over one ridiculous post?

307
00:21:15,320 --> 00:21:20,700
A post, that got 1.1 million views.
This is becoming a huge deal...

308
00:21:20,700 --> 00:21:24,370
Does everyone in the world
have nothing better to do?

309
00:21:24,370 --> 00:21:26,990
I wish I could request a disclosure.

310
00:21:26,990 --> 00:21:27,800
We don't have the budget.

311
00:21:27,800 --> 00:21:29,620
- I know!
- Sorry, ma'am.

312
00:21:29,620 --> 00:21:34,650
Ms. Akutsu, you and I will deal with the PTA.

313
00:21:34,650 --> 00:21:35,500
Right.

314
00:21:35,500 --> 00:21:39,740
Mr. Majima, Ms. Nashiki.
Please prepare for anything else to come.

315
00:21:39,740 --> 00:21:41,400
- Alright.
- Yes, ma'am.

316
00:21:41,400 --> 00:21:43,660
Okay. Shall we?

317
00:21:43,660 --> 00:21:45,400
Excuse us.

318
00:21:49,840 --> 00:21:55,260
School is so tiring because of
all these unexpected events.

319
00:21:55,260 --> 00:21:58,160
But the Principal has no ill will.

320
00:21:58,160 --> 00:21:59,780
She has her own things to protect.

321
00:21:59,780 --> 00:22:01,120
Right.

322
00:22:01,820 --> 00:22:05,870
She's only trying
to protect the whole school.

323
00:22:05,870 --> 00:22:08,230
And she's not wrong to do so.

324
00:22:10,170 --> 00:22:12,290
The school's better off without me.

325
00:22:12,290 --> 00:22:14,660
That's not true.

326
00:22:14,660 --> 00:22:18,280
I find your opinions quite refreshing.

327
00:22:18,280 --> 00:22:20,980
You always have a different way
of seeing things.

328
00:22:20,980 --> 00:22:24,540
That's because I know no common sense.

329
00:22:24,540 --> 00:22:28,030
Which means
you're not restricted by it either.

330
00:22:28,640 --> 00:22:30,370
I think that's rather nice.

331
00:22:32,260 --> 00:22:34,360
I really do.

332
00:22:35,280 --> 00:22:38,820
Th-Thank you.

333
00:22:44,990 --> 00:22:46,310
Ms. Nashiki.

334
00:22:47,810 --> 00:22:52,480
Would you like to join me for some Italian?

335
00:22:55,090 --> 00:22:56,830
I mean, rather than head home alone...

336
00:22:56,830 --> 00:22:58,050
Mr. Majima.

337
00:22:58,050 --> 00:22:59,390
Yes?

338
00:22:59,930 --> 00:23:02,360
What's an "Italian"?

339
00:23:02,360 --> 00:23:03,760
Huh?

340
00:23:04,270 --> 00:23:07,200
There you are, Iroha!

341
00:23:07,200 --> 00:23:09,470
Did you just get off work?

342
00:23:09,470 --> 00:23:10,780
Yes.

343
00:23:12,340 --> 00:23:14,220
Oh my, sorry to interrupt.

344
00:23:14,220 --> 00:23:17,510
Iroha has plans with me tonight.

345
00:23:17,510 --> 00:23:19,400
Is that so?

346
00:23:19,400 --> 00:23:21,060
I'm sorry, I had no idea.

347
00:23:21,060 --> 00:23:21,980
No, it's fine.

348
00:23:21,980 --> 00:23:24,050
Don't worry, it happens.

349
00:23:24,050 --> 00:23:28,370
But see, Iroha and I have something
very important to discuss today.

350
00:23:28,370 --> 00:23:30,480
Important?

351
00:23:31,420 --> 00:23:34,280
Something we'd rather keep on the down low.

352
00:23:35,210 --> 00:23:36,510
Alright.

353
00:23:36,510 --> 00:23:38,580
In that case, I'll see you again tomorrow.

354
00:23:38,580 --> 00:23:40,880
- Mr. Majima.
- Don't worry.

355
00:23:40,880 --> 00:23:43,730
I keep my secrets.

356
00:23:43,730 --> 00:23:45,070
Enjoy yourselves.

357
00:23:49,660 --> 00:23:51,940
What does he know?

358
00:23:51,940 --> 00:23:55,510
Beats me. But he's a great teacher.

359
00:23:55,510 --> 00:23:57,360
I can see that.

360
00:24:05,580 --> 00:24:07,090
This is good.

361
00:24:09,750 --> 00:24:11,440
Do you eat at places like this with Ikuru?

362
00:24:11,440 --> 00:24:14,550
No, no. This is a first for me.

363
00:24:14,550 --> 00:24:16,650
Thank you for waiting.

364
00:24:16,650 --> 00:24:18,270
Here are your Porgy carpaccios,

365
00:24:18,270 --> 00:24:20,040
your Fruit and tomato insalatas.

366
00:24:20,040 --> 00:24:21,770
Your Shrimp and garlic ajillos,

367
00:24:21,770 --> 00:24:22,720
and All'arrabbiatas.

368
00:24:22,720 --> 00:24:25,110
Thank you. Please bring the rest as well.

369
00:24:25,110 --> 00:24:27,000
Yes, ma'am.

370
00:24:28,040 --> 00:24:31,710
Are these all foreign foods?

371
00:24:36,730 --> 00:24:38,360
What a smell!

372
00:24:38,360 --> 00:24:39,540
Go on, dig in.

373
00:24:39,540 --> 00:24:41,070
Thank you.

374
00:24:57,570 --> 00:24:59,570
That's delicious!

375
00:25:02,560 --> 00:25:06,090
I've never tasted something like this before.

376
00:25:06,090 --> 00:25:08,470
Wow, that's good.

377
00:25:08,470 --> 00:25:13,090
You don't seem like one of the bad ones.

378
00:25:14,450 --> 00:25:19,100
Rinka? What was that?

379
00:25:19,100 --> 00:25:21,070
I'm sorry, I don't think I follow.

380
00:25:22,400 --> 00:25:25,830
Oh, so you're not conscious of it yourself.

381
00:25:25,830 --> 00:25:26,700
Huh?

382
00:25:26,700 --> 00:25:30,530
So, how did you meet Ikuru?

383
00:25:33,310 --> 00:25:36,200
He was sort of there when I arrived.

384
00:25:36,200 --> 00:25:40,730
Ikuru and Saya say I'm a...

385
00:25:41,820 --> 00:25:44,670
A time traveler.

386
00:25:45,810 --> 00:25:47,880
What? A time traveler?

387
00:25:47,880 --> 00:25:50,360
It's apparently a thing.

388
00:25:50,360 --> 00:25:52,590
Oh my gosh.

389
00:25:54,320 --> 00:25:56,360
Are you okay?

390
00:25:58,090 --> 00:26:00,280
You're so kind.

391
00:26:05,140 --> 00:26:09,320
So? Why are you possessing her?

392
00:26:10,090 --> 00:26:11,880
Huh?

393
00:26:11,880 --> 00:26:15,910
You're dead.

394
00:26:20,370 --> 00:26:21,940
So?

395
00:26:21,940 --> 00:26:24,460
Why are you possessing her?

396
00:26:25,330 --> 00:26:26,660
Huh?

397
00:26:27,190 --> 00:26:31,220
You're dead.

398
00:26:35,310 --> 00:26:39,500
I'm... dead?

399
00:26:40,080 --> 00:26:41,630
Yes.

400
00:26:46,050 --> 00:26:47,850
But I'm moving just fine.

401
00:26:47,850 --> 00:26:50,400
That's because you're in a vessel.

402
00:26:50,970 --> 00:26:56,970
You've begun to realize, haven't you?

403
00:27:00,060 --> 00:27:02,030
Rinka...

404
00:27:03,030 --> 00:27:04,970
What are you?

405
00:27:05,990 --> 00:27:09,130
In matches, people call me "the Devil".

406
00:27:12,400 --> 00:27:14,390
Now, let's eat.

407
00:27:21,460 --> 00:27:26,160
I don't know why I'm here myself.

408
00:27:30,660 --> 00:27:33,840
I don't remember dying either.

409
00:27:34,700 --> 00:27:39,570
I can't remember anything.

410
00:27:39,570 --> 00:27:41,450
All I want

411
00:27:43,140 --> 00:27:45,900
is to protect Ikuru.

412
00:27:47,310 --> 00:27:50,760
Ikuru and I are childhood friends.

413
00:27:50,760 --> 00:27:53,680
I moved into his neighborhood
when I was younger.

414
00:27:53,680 --> 00:27:55,060
Excuse me...

415
00:27:56,320 --> 00:27:58,420
I want to get off.

416
00:28:00,120 --> 00:28:01,970
Please, stop.

417
00:28:01,970 --> 00:28:03,980
Wait.

418
00:28:13,360 --> 00:28:15,400
Let's go home.

419
00:28:18,800 --> 00:28:23,410
<i>He always cleared the air around me.
He's someone very important to me.</i>

420
00:28:23,410 --> 00:28:25,190
Okay!

421
00:28:25,190 --> 00:28:29,020
So if you're aiming for Ikuru,

422
00:28:29,720 --> 00:28:32,770
then I'll exorcise you, no questions asked.

423
00:28:32,770 --> 00:28:39,340
Rinka... You can get rid of me?

424
00:28:42,010 --> 00:28:45,860
Even if you tell me I'm dead,

425
00:28:45,860 --> 00:28:48,170
I don't know what to do about it.

426
00:28:48,730 --> 00:28:52,370
I really can't remember a thing.

427
00:28:53,360 --> 00:28:54,870
But...

428
00:28:56,450 --> 00:29:03,230
I can't allow myself to take control
of someone else's life any longer.

429
00:29:05,520 --> 00:29:10,890
You really aren't an evil soul, are you?

430
00:29:15,540 --> 00:29:17,130
I lied.

431
00:29:17,130 --> 00:29:18,100
Huh?

432
00:29:18,100 --> 00:29:20,350
I can't exorcise you.

433
00:29:20,350 --> 00:29:24,010
Especially when you're such an old spirit.

434
00:29:24,910 --> 00:29:28,250
But I might be able to help.

435
00:29:28,250 --> 00:29:30,420
Help?

436
00:29:35,470 --> 00:29:39,500
Could we get a Rosemary-honey panna cotta,
a Blood orange granita,

437
00:29:39,500 --> 00:29:41,650
- and herb teas for us both?
- Of course.

438
00:29:41,650 --> 00:29:43,290
Thank you.

439
00:29:44,740 --> 00:29:47,340
I ordered plenty for us.

440
00:29:47,340 --> 00:29:49,170
So let's share, okay?

441
00:30:07,770 --> 00:30:09,990
To find the area,

442
00:30:09,990 --> 00:30:15,180
you flip this triangle, and attach it there.

443
00:30:15,180 --> 00:30:16,830
Give it a try.

444
00:30:19,140 --> 00:30:21,450
See? Now it's become a square.

445
00:30:21,450 --> 00:30:23,210
So now what do you do?

446
00:30:24,250 --> 00:30:25,590
7 x 7 equals 49?

447
00:30:25,590 --> 00:30:27,790
Correct! Great job!

448
00:30:27,790 --> 00:30:28,740
Teach us more!

449
00:30:28,740 --> 00:30:31,040
Sorry, that's all the time I got.

450
00:30:31,040 --> 00:30:34,710
I'm helping Master Seki teach today.

451
00:30:34,710 --> 00:30:36,940
It's the one day I get to be a teacher!

452
00:30:36,940 --> 00:30:38,370
See you kids later!

453
00:30:47,080 --> 00:30:49,080
You rats!

454
00:30:49,080 --> 00:30:51,020
- I'll kill you all!
- Sir, please!

455
00:30:51,020 --> 00:30:54,010
Forgive them! They are only children!

456
00:30:54,010 --> 00:30:55,370
They meant no harm!

457
00:30:55,370 --> 00:30:56,450
Please, forgive us!

458
00:30:56,450 --> 00:30:58,110
Move!

459
00:30:58,110 --> 00:30:59,600
Scram!

460
00:31:02,160 --> 00:31:03,570
Run!

461
00:31:03,570 --> 00:31:05,810
- Run!
- Go, go!

462
00:31:05,810 --> 00:31:09,030
You damn woman!

463
00:31:11,750 --> 00:31:15,040
Get back here!

464
00:31:15,040 --> 00:31:17,600
<i>If I escape to Hachiman Shrine...</i>

465
00:31:24,530 --> 00:31:25,820
<i>I will solve your equation.</i>

466
00:31:25,820 --> 00:31:29,070
I will answer whatever question you give me.

467
00:31:29,070 --> 00:31:30,620
It is a promise.

468
00:31:30,620 --> 00:31:32,740
<i>Mathematics will always tie us together.</i>

469
00:31:32,740 --> 00:31:34,670
<i>Almost there!</i>

470
00:32:00,540 --> 00:32:02,510
Our...

471
00:32:03,590 --> 00:32:05,340
Our promise...

472
00:32:31,520 --> 00:32:34,580
- <i>I still wanna live.</i>
- <i>I still want to live.</i>

473
00:33:02,600 --> 00:33:05,780
Let's have something sweet, okay?

474
00:33:12,630 --> 00:33:16,350
Ichika, did you figure out
who stole your stickers?

475
00:33:17,950 --> 00:33:19,400
No.

476
00:33:19,400 --> 00:33:22,790
What? Aren't your teachers looking into it?

477
00:33:22,790 --> 00:33:24,790
And what about the thief?

478
00:33:25,340 --> 00:33:26,990
Unbelievable.

479
00:33:26,990 --> 00:33:31,310
Does the school not care
that there's been a crime?

480
00:33:31,310 --> 00:33:34,150
There's a criminal in your very class!

481
00:33:34,150 --> 00:33:35,700
Mom?

482
00:33:35,700 --> 00:33:37,190
This is ridiculous.

483
00:33:37,190 --> 00:33:40,240
Offenders need to be punished.

484
00:33:40,240 --> 00:33:41,530
All who disagree are wrong.

485
00:33:41,530 --> 00:33:43,650
Why can't anyone see that?

486
00:33:43,650 --> 00:33:45,240
Mom.

487
00:33:46,340 --> 00:33:48,590
That hurts.

488
00:33:48,590 --> 00:33:51,360
Get going, you'll be late.

489
00:33:51,930 --> 00:33:53,310
Okay.

490
00:33:54,430 --> 00:33:57,390
- Good morning.
- Morning.

491
00:34:02,480 --> 00:34:04,280
- Ms. Nashiki.
- Morning.

492
00:34:04,280 --> 00:34:07,740
I have questions. What's all this about?

493
00:34:09,890 --> 00:34:11,460
I stayed with Rinka yesterday night.

494
00:34:11,460 --> 00:34:14,050
What? Why were you with Rinka?

495
00:34:14,050 --> 00:34:16,390
I had some of that "Italian" stuff, too.

496
00:34:16,390 --> 00:34:18,010
Not what I asked.

497
00:34:18,010 --> 00:34:20,700
- How'd you get that?
- Rinka sent it to me.

498
00:34:20,700 --> 00:34:21,750
Good morning.

499
00:34:21,750 --> 00:34:24,160
Good morning.

500
00:34:24,160 --> 00:34:25,640
I heard you talking about Italian food.

501
00:34:25,640 --> 00:34:27,620
You know of it?

502
00:34:27,620 --> 00:34:29,250
So, is everything okay?

503
00:34:29,250 --> 00:34:31,230
Sure is.

504
00:34:31,230 --> 00:34:33,580
I'm glad I bumped into you.

505
00:34:33,580 --> 00:34:35,560
Let's work hard, yeah?

506
00:34:35,560 --> 00:34:37,340
What gives?

507
00:34:38,850 --> 00:34:40,420
Flirting this early?

508
00:34:40,420 --> 00:34:42,540
No, sir!

509
00:34:45,570 --> 00:34:46,820
Ichika, let's walk home together!

510
00:34:46,820 --> 00:34:49,010
We'll wait at the gate, okay?

511
00:34:55,410 --> 00:34:57,130
Ichika.

512
00:34:59,000 --> 00:35:01,280
Your Ramunen stickers...

513
00:35:02,800 --> 00:35:04,790
I didn't steal them.

514
00:35:07,310 --> 00:35:10,080
<i>I did want them.</i>

515
00:35:10,080 --> 00:35:13,610
<i>But it wasn't me.</i>

516
00:35:14,220 --> 00:35:17,440
I want to stay friends with you, Ichika.

517
00:35:18,300 --> 00:35:22,620
Will you believe me? Please?

518
00:35:23,820 --> 00:35:25,170
Yeah.

519
00:35:25,860 --> 00:35:27,560
I know it wasn't you.

520
00:35:29,650 --> 00:35:33,820
Those stickers really are super rare.

521
00:35:34,350 --> 00:35:37,590
No one else should have them, but...

522
00:35:37,590 --> 00:35:40,260
- Where'd you get those?
- My mom got them for me.

523
00:35:40,260 --> 00:35:42,360
Cute, right?

524
00:35:42,360 --> 00:35:44,180
And look at this.

525
00:35:44,180 --> 00:35:46,120
You have Ramunen stickers, too?

526
00:35:46,120 --> 00:35:47,810
They're so cute!

527
00:35:47,810 --> 00:35:51,450
I'll trade you three pages for one sticker!

528
00:35:52,010 --> 00:35:53,830
- Sure!
- Really?

529
00:35:53,830 --> 00:35:57,990
- Aw, lucky.
- I want one, too!

530
00:35:57,990 --> 00:36:01,060
Why did this have to happen?

531
00:36:09,340 --> 00:36:10,840
Everything okay?

532
00:36:12,370 --> 00:36:14,740
Is Ms. Nashiki here?

533
00:36:14,740 --> 00:36:16,520
Huh?

534
00:36:16,520 --> 00:36:18,000
Here.

535
00:36:20,930 --> 00:36:23,780
We want you to hold onto these for us.

536
00:36:23,780 --> 00:36:25,440
Huh?

537
00:36:25,440 --> 00:36:27,190
Why?

538
00:36:28,000 --> 00:36:30,230
Aren't these important to you two?

539
00:36:30,230 --> 00:36:34,020
We don't want to hold onto them anymore.

540
00:36:34,020 --> 00:36:37,340
Ms. Nashiki, can you come talk to my mom?

541
00:36:45,350 --> 00:36:46,860
<i>Yes?</i>

542
00:36:46,860 --> 00:36:48,520
Good afternoon.

543
00:36:48,520 --> 00:36:52,430
I am Ichika's homeroom teacher, Nashiki.

544
00:36:59,500 --> 00:37:00,880
Ichika...

545
00:37:00,880 --> 00:37:02,150
Mom...

546
00:37:02,150 --> 00:37:04,000
Why have you come unannounced?

547
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:06,390
Did you find the thief?

548
00:37:06,390 --> 00:37:07,800
No, ma'am.

549
00:37:08,850 --> 00:37:14,610
Ichika has requested
not to press the case any further.

550
00:37:16,290 --> 00:37:17,970
Why not?

551
00:37:17,970 --> 00:37:20,600
Your belongings were stolen.

552
00:37:20,600 --> 00:37:23,750
Why does the victim
have to accept things as they are?

553
00:37:23,750 --> 00:37:28,420
Ichika wishes to return
to when she had no stickers at all.

554
00:37:31,530 --> 00:37:34,830
You made that decision without my permission?

555
00:37:34,830 --> 00:37:36,490
I see.

556
00:37:37,360 --> 00:37:39,260
So you'll betray me, too.

557
00:37:39,260 --> 00:37:42,610
No! Mom, that's not it!

558
00:37:43,120 --> 00:37:44,840
Mrs. Nakamura.

559
00:37:44,840 --> 00:37:48,870
School is not a place for you
to vent your frustrations at.

560
00:37:49,900 --> 00:37:53,800
Ichika will choose how to lead her own life.

561
00:37:53,800 --> 00:37:58,130
No one can live her life for her.

562
00:37:58,850 --> 00:38:01,450
What would you know?

563
00:38:01,450 --> 00:38:03,270
You're just her homeroom teacher!

564
00:38:03,270 --> 00:38:06,390
I apologize for my brazen remark.

565
00:38:06,390 --> 00:38:11,480
I will give all my kids a warning
regarding this incident.

566
00:38:12,200 --> 00:38:14,800
So much as I am allowed, that is.

567
00:38:16,440 --> 00:38:18,310
Ichika, get inside.

568
00:38:19,220 --> 00:38:21,390
And you, please leave.

569
00:38:39,360 --> 00:38:44,360
<i>If only I were Ms. Nashiki.</i>

570
00:38:56,290 --> 00:38:59,790
<i>No one can live her life for her.</i>

571
00:39:06,410 --> 00:39:08,600
Good evening.

572
00:39:08,600 --> 00:39:10,250
- Evening.
- Huh?

573
00:39:10,250 --> 00:39:11,750
You're the only one left?

574
00:39:11,750 --> 00:39:13,010
Where are the others?

575
00:39:13,010 --> 00:39:14,980
They've all gone home.

576
00:39:14,980 --> 00:39:16,440
I see.

577
00:39:22,800 --> 00:39:24,430
- Ms. Nashiki.
- Hm?

578
00:39:24,430 --> 00:39:28,090
Visitations without prior scheduling
are not allowed.

579
00:39:28,090 --> 00:39:29,480
Right.

580
00:39:31,010 --> 00:39:33,580
I just walked a student home.

581
00:39:34,170 --> 00:39:38,070
If you meddle any further in this case,
you will be fired.

582
00:39:38,070 --> 00:39:39,350
Yeah.

583
00:39:40,480 --> 00:39:45,920
But if a student asks for my help,

584
00:39:45,920 --> 00:39:47,940
I think I'll end up taking action.

585
00:39:48,730 --> 00:39:52,310
I know you will.

586
00:39:53,500 --> 00:39:56,400
So? Did you manage to speak to her guardian?

587
00:39:56,400 --> 00:39:57,800
Yeah.

588
00:40:00,210 --> 00:40:02,150
I was a bit rude to her.

589
00:40:02,150 --> 00:40:04,340
What? You got into an argument
with her mother?

590
00:40:04,340 --> 00:40:06,520
No, it didn't escalate that far.

591
00:40:06,520 --> 00:40:08,650
It didn't, but...

592
00:40:09,930 --> 00:40:11,650
What did you say to her?

593
00:40:14,200 --> 00:40:17,560
I told her to quit using school
as a way to vent her frustrations.

594
00:40:17,560 --> 00:40:19,600
She turned me away immediately.

595
00:40:21,900 --> 00:40:23,870
Next time, I'm coming with you.

596
00:40:23,870 --> 00:40:27,380
Hey, I did my job as a homeroom teacher.

597
00:40:27,380 --> 00:40:28,760
I helped a kid when they asked for it.

598
00:40:28,760 --> 00:40:32,000
Making enemies out of their parents
doesn't help anyone though.

599
00:40:32,000 --> 00:40:33,690
I'm glad she shut the door on you.

600
00:40:33,690 --> 00:40:35,390
Better that, than getting into a fight.

601
00:40:35,390 --> 00:40:37,860
Ikuru, you don't trust me at all.

602
00:40:37,860 --> 00:40:40,490
I'd never raise my hand at a parent.

603
00:40:40,490 --> 00:40:43,680
I'm starting to pick up on modern cues.

604
00:40:43,680 --> 00:40:47,340
I even ate that "Italian" stuff.

605
00:40:47,340 --> 00:40:51,060
Right, I still had things to say about that.

606
00:40:51,060 --> 00:40:54,400
Why are you getting dinner with Rinka,
before eating out with me?

607
00:40:55,570 --> 00:40:57,320
Because we're friends?

608
00:40:57,320 --> 00:40:59,410
Friends?

609
00:41:00,200 --> 00:41:01,820
But we're better acquainted with each other.

610
00:41:01,820 --> 00:41:04,770
Of course we are.

611
00:41:04,770 --> 00:41:08,330
You look after me, right?

612
00:41:14,980 --> 00:41:17,140
- Ms. Nashiki.
- Yeah?

613
00:41:17,790 --> 00:41:21,910
I would like to update
our relationship status.

614
00:41:21,910 --> 00:41:23,760
What do you mean?

615
00:41:23,760 --> 00:41:27,860
I'm not just someone
who happens to look after you.

616
00:41:27,860 --> 00:41:30,960
I'd like us to be something more.

617
00:41:31,850 --> 00:41:34,170
- Tea-drinking buddies?
- No!

618
00:41:34,170 --> 00:41:37,930
Besides, we already eat together.
Not the Italian, though.

619
00:41:37,930 --> 00:41:40,590
Sorry, I bet you wanted to try some, too.

620
00:41:40,590 --> 00:41:42,320
It was delicious!

621
00:41:42,320 --> 00:41:44,950
I don't care.

622
00:41:45,530 --> 00:41:47,570
I mean, I do, but...

623
00:41:52,750 --> 00:41:56,580
Let's go again, together.

624
00:41:57,940 --> 00:42:00,180
As a date.

625
00:42:00,180 --> 00:42:02,150
A "date"?

626
00:42:03,410 --> 00:42:05,770
It means to go out together. More precisely,

627
00:42:05,770 --> 00:42:11,680
so two individuals who feel a certain way,
can get... closer.

628
00:42:16,340 --> 00:42:18,420
You mean...

629
00:42:20,220 --> 00:42:21,900
a rendezvous?

630
00:42:21,900 --> 00:42:23,420
A rendezvous?

631
00:42:26,960 --> 00:42:30,920
Yes. I'm inviting you on a rendezvous.

632
00:42:47,390 --> 00:42:48,800
Sorry.

633
00:42:49,630 --> 00:42:52,280
I can't. Not for a while.

634
00:42:52,280 --> 00:42:53,860
Huh?

635
00:42:53,860 --> 00:42:58,350
See, I've gotten a hang
of this teacher work now.

636
00:42:58,350 --> 00:43:00,980
So I want to try making it on my own.

637
00:43:00,980 --> 00:43:02,110
I'm not following.

638
00:43:02,110 --> 00:43:05,950
I'll live as Iroha Nashiki.
She's got a place for herself.

639
00:43:05,950 --> 00:43:07,760
You're leaving my apartment?

640
00:43:07,760 --> 00:43:10,110
- Yeah.
- What?

641
00:43:10,110 --> 00:43:13,220
- You can't get by on your own!
- That's not your decision to make!

642
00:43:17,690 --> 00:43:21,170
Ikuru. You said so yourself, remember?

643
00:43:21,170 --> 00:43:23,150
That once I settle down, I'd move out.

644
00:43:23,150 --> 00:43:24,380
Well...

645
00:43:24,380 --> 00:43:28,210
I've gotten a hang of things here.

646
00:43:28,210 --> 00:43:31,360
I can't be in your care forever.

647
00:43:36,250 --> 00:43:38,150
<i>Rinka.</i>

648
00:43:38,150 --> 00:43:40,160
I need to ask you a favor.

649
00:43:40,830 --> 00:43:43,400
Please don't tell Ikuru about this.

650
00:43:44,120 --> 00:43:46,150
Not until the day I disappear.

651
00:43:51,380 --> 00:43:55,100
I'll be moving back to Iroha Nashiki's place.

652
00:43:55,100 --> 00:43:57,360
Alone? How? There's no way...

653
00:43:57,360 --> 00:43:59,010
I'll be fine alone.

654
00:43:59,010 --> 00:44:00,420
See you.

655
00:44:06,570 --> 00:44:10,060
But why?

656
00:45:04,600 --> 00:45:06,680
<i>You're allowed to stay.</i>

657
00:45:42,290 --> 00:45:44,010
Ms. Nashiki?

658
00:45:45,890 --> 00:45:48,480
Mr. Majima...

659
00:45:48,480 --> 00:45:50,110
What's wrong?

660
00:45:51,960 --> 00:45:53,970
You're crying...
